  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of member States to ratify and apply international labour standards and to fulfil their reporting obligations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO undertook a High-Level Mission (HLM) to Zimbabwe in 2017, as guided by the ILO ILC Committee on the Application of Standards (CAS). A Plan of Action for the implementation of recommendations of the ILO International Labour Conference (ILC) Committee on the Application of Standards (CAS) High Level Mission was adopted Government and Social Partners in February 2017. In April 2022 a Direct Contact Mission was conducted and an Action Plan was developed to address the issues raised by the tripartite partners. 
Labour law reforms (Health Services Act, Public Service Act, , OSH Bill) are ongoing, a synthesis of ILO supervisory bodies&#x2019; comments and observation on Zimbabwe&#x2019;s implementation of its international labour standards, has established gaps for legislative reforms. Government and social partners updated the principles for labour law reform to include principles on addressing retrenchments, equal pay for work of equal value, SGBV, and termination of employment. 
The Labour Law Reform Bill was passed in August 2023. The Tripartite Negotiating Forum (TNF) Act was promulgated in May 2019 to ensure that the dialogue and agreements there-off are collective and binding. Due to new and some outstanding issues with ILO supervisory bodies Zimbabwe is expected to report on actions it has taken to give effect to ratified conventions and recommendations of the same. 
Zimbabwe currently has an active National Employment Policy Framework (ZiNEPF), which was developed and adopted in 2010, which unfortunately has been hampered by inadequate implementation and a challenging socio-economic and political context. Following the change in administration in 2017, the Government implemented a national development framework: the Transitional Stabilization Programme (&#x201c;TSP&#x201d;), which ran from October 2018 through December 2020. A number of key national economic and sectoral policies were also expected to be developed in the coming months, and the Ministry of Labour expressed its inte</narrative>
  </description>
